<h2 id=definitions>
Definitions</h2>

<dl>
  <dt><dfn export>Archive</dfn> or <dfn export>Uncompressed Archive</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A logical collection of files, directories, and associated metadata.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Barely Compressible</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A condition where the algorithm overhead from compression exceeds the size saving
  so that the normal Compressed Block can't be used.

  <div class=note>
    By definition, this condition varies by the choice of compression algorithm.
    In the case of Zstandard, the minimum overhead is 3 bytes per Zstandard block,
    each of which holds at most 128 KiB (2<sup>17</sup> bytes) of data.
  </div>
  </dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Bit</dfn></dt>
  <dd>The smallest unit of information, representing a binary value of either 0 or 1.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Byte</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A sequence of 8 bits, typically the smallest addressable unit of data.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Compress</dfn> or <dfn export>Compression</dfn></dt>
  <dd>The process of transforming an uncompressed archive into a compressed archive, typically to reduce its size.
  This is the inverse operation of decompression.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Compressor</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A component or algorithm responsible for performing compression.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Compressed Archive</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A sequence of bytes that represents an archive, organized in a way that is usually, but not necessarily,
  more compact than the naive concatenation of its constituent parts.
  A single uncompressed archive can have multiple valid compressed representations.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Decompress</dfn> or <dfn export>Decompression</dfn></dt>
  <dd>The process of transforming a compressed archive back into its original uncompressed form.
  This is the inverse operation of compression.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Decompressor</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A component or algorithm responsible for performing decompression.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Dictionary</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A byte sequence supplied to the compression method as if it preceded the data,
  typically shared by multiple small blocks to compensate for the lack of shared compression context.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Directory</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A special type of file that contains references to other files and/or directories,
  forming a hierarchical file system structure.
  It includes its own name and associated metadata.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>File</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A fundamental unit of archival, consisting of a sequence of zero or more content bytes,
  a non-empty byte sequence for its name, and any other associated metadata (e.g., size, modification time, permissions).</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Formula</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A description of a file content as an expression over stored data, modules and resources.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Implicitly-sized</dfn></dt>
  <dd>Being its byte length only available through the context.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Module</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A WebAssembly module that performs the postprocessing part of a transformation.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Postprocess</dfn> or <dfn export>Postprocessor</dfn></dt>
  <dd>The act or component that performs operations on data *after* it has been decompressed or transformed,
  but before it is considered fully restored to its original state.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Preprocess</dfn> or <dfn export>Preprocessor</dfn></dt>
  <dd>The act or component that performs operations on data *before* it is compressed or transformed,
  but after it has been retrieved from its original source.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Resource</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A byte sequence shared by multiple invocations of modules, typically a model for the data.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Transform</dfn> or <dfn export>Transformation</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A combined term for matching preprocessing and postprocessing algorithms.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Transformer</dfn></dt>
  <dd>An algorithm responsible for performing transformation.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Zstandard block</dfn></dt>
  <dd>The "Block" structure in the Zstandard frame [[RFC8878]], Section 3.1.1.2.</dd>
</dl>

<h2 id=conventions>
Conventions</h2>

<h3 id=expressions>
Expressions</h3>

All expressions are interpreted according to the standard arithmetics, with the following extensions:

*   |a| / |b| equals to floor(|a| ÷ |b|).
*   |a| % |b| is a non-negative modulo of |a| divided by |b|, and equals to |a| - (|a| / |b|) × |b|.
*   |a| &lt;&lt; |b| equals to |a| × 2<sup>|b|</sup>.
*   |a| &gt;&gt; |b| equals to |a| / 2<sup>|b|</sup>.
*   |XX|<sub>16</sub> denotes a hexadecimal integer.
*   (|a|, |b|, …) denotes an ordered array of values.
*   (|XX| |YY| |ZZ|)<sub>16</sub> is a shorthand for (|XX|<sub>16</sub>, |YY|<sub>16</sub>, |ZZ|<sub>16</sub>).
*   |a| || |b| concatenates two arrays |a| and |b|, treating any non-array value as a singleton array (|a|) or (|b|).
*   For array |a|, |a|<sup>|n|</sup> equals to |a| || |a|<sup>|n|-1</sup> when |n| > 0.
    |a|<sup>0</sup> equals to an empty array ().
*   [|a|, |b|] denotes a closed interval between |a| and |b|, both inclusive.
*   [|a|, |b|) denotes a half-open interval between |a| (inclusive) and |b| (exclusive).

All expressions result in an integer between -(2<sup>64</sup> - 1) and 2<sup>64</sup> - 1,
or an array of those integers with the number of elements not exceeding 2<sup>64</sup> - 1.
Note that subexpressions still may exceed this range.

<h3 id=types>
Types</h3>

Types are a set of values and associated encoding scheme.
Unless stated as being [=implicitly-sized=],
all types have the defined byte length out of the context.

<dfn for=type>bool</dfn> is a Boolean value, either true or false.
It has no associated encoding scheme and can't occur in any structure.

<dfn for=type>u8</dfn> is an unsigned 8-bit integer, occupying 1 byte.
Its range is [0, 2<sup>8</sup>).

<dfn for=type>u16le</dfn> is an unsigned 16-bit integer, occupying 2 bytes,
stored in little-endian byte order (least significant byte first).
Its range is [0, 2<sup>16</sup>).

<dfn for=type>u32le</dfn> is an unsigned 32-bit integer, occupying 4 bytes,
stored in little-endian byte order.
Its range is [0, 2<sup>32</sup>).

<dfn for=type>u64le</dfn> is an unsigned 64-bit integer, occupying 8 bytes,
stored in little-endian byte order.
Its range is [0, 2<sup>64</sup>).

<strong><dfn for=type>u</dfn>(|n|)</strong>, where |n| is an integer less than 2<sup>64</sup>,
is the smallest integer type that can represent |n| if |n| > 0,
and a zero-sized type if |n| = 0.
In the other words,

* If |n| = 0, u(|n|) is zero-sized.
* If |n| ∈ [1, 2<sup>8</sup>), u(|n|) is same as [=u8=].
* If |n| ∈ [2<sup>8</sup>, 2<sup>16</sup>), u(|n|) is same as [=u16le=].
* If |n| ∈ [2<sup>16</sup>, 2<sup>32</sup>), u(|n|) is same as [=u32le=].
* If |n| ∈ [2<sup>32</sup>, 2<sup>64</sup>), u(|n|) is same as [=u64le=].

<dfn for=type>leb128</dfn> is an unsigned 64-bit integer encoded using the variable number of bytes with the LEB128 encoding.
Each byte, except the last, has its most significant bit (MSB) set to 1, indicating that more bytes follow.
The remaining 7 bits of each byte are used to store the integer's value.
The least significant 7 bits of the first byte are the least significant bits of the integer, and so on.
The last byte has its MSB set to 0.
Its range is [0, 2<sup>64</sup>).

<div class=note>
  This allows for multiple encodings for given number for any number smaller than 2<sup>32</sup>.
</div>

<strong>|a|[|n|]</strong>, where |n| is an integer, denotes an ordered array of *a*s.
|n|'s range is [0, 2<sup>64</sup>).

<strong>|a|[*]</strong> denotes an [=implicitly-sized=] unordered array of *a*s with the unspecified quantity.
The number of elements is implicitly determined from the context.
This specification uses two kinds of such contexts:

*   When one or more |a|[*] fields are in the structure with the defined byte length,
    the numbers of elements in those fields are increased as needed to exactly fill the required byte length.
    There MUST be a single set of the numbers of elements that satisfy these conditions.
*   When an |a|[*] field is given with a constraint,
    the field extends until the first instance of |a| which doesn't satisfy the required constraint.

<dfn for=type>string</dfn> is an alias of [=u8=][*] with an additional constraint that
the byte sequence is a valid UTF-8 string. [[RFC3629]]

<h3 id=structure>
Structure</h3>

**Structure** is a logical group of one or more ordered **fields** of possibly differing types concatenated together.
Each field has the following properties:

* The descriptive **name** of the field (e.g. [=block/size=], [=cdata/compression_params=]).
* The data **type** of the field (e.g. [=leb128=], [=u8=][size]), with an optional constraint.
* Optional criteria or **conditions** that dictate the presence, value, or interpretation of the field.

Field names can only coincide when the field's type varies by conditions,
in which case at most one such condition is applicable at any time.
The name can be omitted when the field's name is same as the previous field.

The field type may be <dfn for=type>offset</dfn>,
which is a special zero-sized type interpreted as
the offset to the following byte relative to the unspecified file position (origin).
Offsets are only used to measure the number of bytes within a set of fields.
The syntax and semantics of this specification don't depend on the choice of origin.

The optional constraint does not affect the field's encoding and only narrows down the possible set of values. Constraints can be one of the following:

*   |a| ∈ |X| requires that the value is in the specified set |X|.
*   |a| ∉ |X| requires that the value is not in the specified set |X|.
*   |a| = |x| requires that the value is exactly the given value |x|.
    If |x| is a constant expression, this effectively fixes the byte encoding of this field.
*   |a| ≠ |x| requires that the value is not the given value |x|.

The conditions may be one of the following:

* "<dfn for=cond>Optional</dfn>" indicates that conforming implementations MAY include or exclude given field as needed.
* "<dfn for=cond>If</dfn> …" indicates that the field MUST be included only when the specified condition is true.
* "<dfn for=cond>Depends on</dfn> …" indicates that the field's interpretation varies with the specified set of other fields.

Structures are [=implicitly-sized=] only when its byte length is not available through the context
and at least one [=implicitly-sized=] field exists therein.

<h3 id=constants>
Constants</h3>

This specification defines and uses the following constants, all of which are denoted in UPPERCASE.

<table class=data dfn-for=const>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Value
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>HEADER_TYPE</dfn>
      <td>278<sub>16</sub>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>FOOTER_TYPE</dfn>
      <td>98&thinsp;68EB&thinsp;D5FD<sub>16</sub>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>ENCRYPTED_HEADER_TYPE</dfn>
      <td>15D&thinsp;14FC&thinsp;E5F9<sub>16</sub>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>HEADER_MAGIC</dfn>
      <td>(F8&nbsp;04&nbsp;03&nbsp;6A&nbsp;D6&nbsp;BE)<sub>16</sub>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>FOOTER_MAGIC</dfn>
      <td>(FD&nbsp;AA&nbsp;AE&nbsp;DE&nbsp;85&nbsp;12)<sub>16</sub>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>ENCRYPTED_HEADER_MAGIC</dfn>
      <td>(F9&nbsp;CA&nbsp;F2&nbsp;A6&nbsp;D0&nbsp;2A&nbsp;01&nbsp;00)<sub>16</sub>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>MIRROR_MAGIC</dfn>
      <td>(02&nbsp;AA&nbsp;F5&nbsp;FE&nbsp;BA&nbsp;2B&nbsp;AE&nbsp;23)<sub>16</sub>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class=note>
  The encoding of [=const/HEADER_TYPE=], [=const/FOOTER_TYPE=] and [=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_TYPE=] as a block type ([[#block]])
  is a prefix of [=const/HEADER_MAGIC=], [=const/FOOTER_MAGIC=] and [=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_MAGIC=] respectively.
</div>

<h2 id=syntax>
Syntax</h2>

<h3 id=block>
Block</h3>

**Blocks** are a typed byte sequence.
The entirety of an unencrypted archive and the beginning of an encrypted archive consists of either blocks or block-like structures.

Blocks have the following generic structure:

<div dfn-for=block>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>block_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>type</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] ≠ [=FOOTER_TYPE=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>size</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=]
      <td>[=If=] [=block/type=] % 4 = 0
    <tr>
      <td>
      <td>[=u16le=]
      <td>[=If=] [=block/type=] % 4 = 1
    <tr>
      <td>
      <td>[=u32le=]
      <td>[=If=] [=block/type=] % 4 = 2
    <tr>
      <td>
      <td>[=u64le=]
      <td>[=If=] [=block/type=] % 4 = 3
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=block/size=]]
      <td>[=Depends on=] [=block/type=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>block_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>padding</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=]
      <td>[=If=] ([=block_end=] - [=block_start=]) % 2 = 1
  </tbody>
</table>

Some [=block/type=] values, including [=FOOTER_TYPE=], are also used to denote block-like structures.
For example the archive footer has to have a distinct block type in order to be placed after blocks.
This specification doesn't assign any meaning to such block-like structures unless explicitly stated.

[=block/type=] MUST use the shortest [=leb128=] encoding.

The presence of [=block/padding=] ensures that there are even number of bytes in any block.
Conforming compressors MUST set [=block/padding=] to zero,
but its value doesn't affect the result of [=interpret an archive|interpreting an archive=].

<div class=note>
  [=block/padding=] carries no information,
  so its damage is not a reason to reject an otherwise readable archive.
  On the other hand, [=block/type=] determines the width of [=block/size=] and where the next block starts,
  so a single encoding for each type leaves no ambiguity in finding block boundaries, e.g. by scanning.
</div>

The following sections describe the contents of [=block/data=] field according to each defined [=block/type=].
Since [=block/type=] also determines the byte length of [=block/size=],
there are generally multiple [=block/type=] values assigned for each logical block type.

</div>

<h4 id=shared-external-structure>
Shared external structure</h4>

Many block types share the same external structure, only varying its internal structure.
It may or may not have been compressed
and may use different <dfn for=block>compression_method</dfn> or <dfn for=block>checksum_method</dfn>
specified by the parent structure.
Each [=checksum_method=] also has an associated, fixed <dfn for=block>checksum_size</dfn>.

The compressed external structure (<dfn for=block>compressed_data</dfn>) consists of the following:

<div dfn-for=cdata>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>original_size_delta</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>compression_params</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>[=Depends on=] [=compression_method=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>checksum</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=checksum_size=]]
      <td>[=If=] [=checksum_method=] is not None, [=depends on=] [=checksum_method=]
  </tbody>
</table>

The compressed size (<dfn>compressed_size</dfn>) is inferred from the block size:
[=cdata/compressed_size=] = [=cdata/data_end=] - [=cdata/data_start=].
The [=cdata/original_size_delta=] field encodes the original size
<dfn>original_size</dfn> = [=cdata/compressed_size=] + [=cdata/original_size_delta=].
Therefore [=cdata/compressed_size=] MUST be equal to or less than [=cdata/original_size=];
any [=barely compressible=] block data MUST NOT use compression.

</div>

The uncompressed external structure (<dfn for=block>uncompressed_data</dfn>) consists of the following:

<div dfn-for=udata>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>checksum</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=checksum_size=]]
      <td>[=If=] [=checksum_method=] is not None
  </tbody>
</table>

</div>

In both structures, the checksum is computed over the [=decoded data=] ([[#block-contents]]),
i.e. the data after decompression, rather than the stored bytes.

<div class=note>
  A checksum over the stored bytes would only verify that they have been read correctly.
  A checksum over the decoded data also verifies the decompression,
  and lets a decompressor reading only some non-leaf blocks verify the file content it has actually read.
</div>

<h5 id=zstandard-compression-method>
Zstandard compression method</h5>

This specification only defines a single compression method, Zstandard [[RFC8878]].
Its parameters ([=cdata/compression_params=]) are as follows:

<div dfn-for=zstd>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>window_descriptor</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

The meaning of the [=window_descriptor=] field is identical to the Zstandard's `Window_Descriptor` byte [[RFC9659]],
which determines `Window_Size` as in [[RFC8878]], Section 3.1.1.1.2.
Consequently each Zstandard block holds at most `Block_Maximum_Size` = min(`Window_Size`, 128 KiB) bytes of data.

Conforming decompressors MUST support any `Window_Size` up to 2<sup>27</sup> bytes (128 MiB),
and MAY return a [=resource failure=] for a larger `Window_Size`.
Conforming compressors SHOULD NOT use a `Window_Size` larger than 2<sup>27</sup> bytes
unless explicitly requested.

If a dictionary is in use, its content, entropy tables and repeat offsets are given by the dictionary block
as described in [[#dictionary-block]].

</div>

Each data compressed by this method is a sequence of Zstandard blocks,
without an additional framing provided by Zstandard frames.
[=cdata/data=] MUST consist of one or more such blocks without any other bytes,
where only the last block is signalled as the final block by its block header.

<div class=note>
  The end of the compressed data is thus given both by [=cdata/data_end=] and by the final block,
  and they have to agree.
</div>

<h5 id=xxh64-checksum-method>
XXH64 checksum method</h5>

This specification only defines a single checksum method, XXH64 [[XXHASH]].
Its seed MUST be zero and the result MUST be stored as [=u64le=].
The resulting [=checksum_size=] is 8.

<h4 id=spacer-block>
Spacer block</h4>

Blocks 0<sub>16</sub> through 3<sub>16</sub> are **spacer blocks**.
Its data consists of the following:

<div dfn-for=spacer>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>space</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=block/size=]]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

</div>

Spacer blocks can skip any number of bytes besides from a single byte,
which is not required due to the presence of blockwise [=block/padding=].

Conforming compressors MUST fill [=spacer/space=] with zero bytes in order to avoid data leak,
but its content doesn't affect the result of [=interpret an archive|interpreting an archive=].
Conforming compressors are strongly RECOMMENDED to utilize sparse files for performance.

<h4 id=size-hint-block>
Size hint block</h4>

Block 4<sub>16</sub> is a **size hint block**.
Its data consists of the following:

<div dfn-for=szhint>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>size_hint</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] ≠ 0
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

If this block is present, it MUST be the first block after the header
and its [=szhint/size_hint=] field MUST be equal to the footer's [=archive/size_hint=] field.

</div>

<h4 id=metadata-block>
Metadata block</h4>

Blocks 10<sub>16</sub> through 17<sub>16</sub> are **metadata blocks**.
Their data uses the Shared external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]]),
with [[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]] as [=checksum_method=]
and [=compression_method=] determined as follows:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Block Types
      <th>[=compression_method=]
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>10<sub>16</sub> — 13<sub>16</sub>
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>14<sub>16</sub> — 17<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class=note>
  Alternatively, the block type can be interpreted as a binary number <code>0001&thinsp;0|m||ss|</code> where:

    * |m| indicates the compression method: 0 for None, 1 for [[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]].
    * |ss| indicates the size of the size field: 0 for [=u8=], 1 for [=u16le=], 2 for [=u32le=], 3 for [=u64le=].
</div>

<div class=note>
  Unlike content blocks, the checksum is mandatory for metadata, dictionary, module and resource blocks.
  Names and the structure of entries are not covered by any other checksum,
  and the damage to these blocks affects many files at once.
  The cost is negligible because these blocks are a small fraction of the archive and each is read only once.
  It also allows a decompressor to detect the damage to these blocks by reading them alone,
  which is a prerequisite for using [[#mirror-block|mirror blocks]].
</div>

The internal structure after decompression is as follows:

<div dfn-for=meta>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>previous_metadata_offset</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_modules</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_resources</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_dictionaries</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_directories</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_files</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_specials</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entries_size</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] = [=meta/entries_end=] - [=meta/entries_start=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>optional_metadata_size</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>module_offsets</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/previous_metadata_offset=])[[=meta/num_modules=]]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>resource_offsets</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/previous_metadata_offset=])[[=meta/num_resources=]]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>dictionary_offsets</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/previous_metadata_offset=])[[=meta/num_dictionaries=]]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entry_parents</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/num_directories=])[[=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=] + [=meta/num_specials=] - 1]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>file_params</dfn>
      <td>[=meta/file_param=][[=meta/num_files=]]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entry_offsets</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/entries_size=])[[=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=] + [=meta/num_specials=] - 1]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entries_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entries</dfn>
      <td>[=meta/entry=][[=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=] + [=meta/num_specials=]]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entries_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>optional_metadata</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=meta/optional_metadata_size=]]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

All relative offsets are measured from the beginning of the current metadata block,
implying that the offset of zero is invalid.
All valid relative offsets are also even.

Each metadata block describes the [=content blocks=] in its [=segment=],
i.e. blocks appeared after the previous metadata block, if any, and until the current metadata block.
The previous metadata block is linked via [=meta/previous_metadata_offset=]
which is also the maximum possible offsets that can occur in this particular metadata block.
If there is no prior metadata block,
[=meta/previous_metadata_offset=] is the relative offset
to the end of [=archive/header_magic=] in the Unencrypted Archive structure ([[#unencrypted-archive]]),
plus 1 so that it never points to a valid block.

[=meta/num_modules=], [=meta/num_resources=] and [=meta/num_dictionaries=] are
the numbers of module, resource and dictionary blocks in the [=segment=] respectively.
Relative offsets to those blocks are
later recorded as [=meta/module_offsets=], [=meta/resource_offsets=] and [=meta/dictionary_offsets=] respectively,
in the order of appearance.

The metadata block represents zero or more directories, zero or more files and zero or more <dfn>special entries</dfn>,
collectively called [=meta/entries=] and numbered together.
The root directory is numbered 0,
subsequent directories are numbered from 1 to [=meta/num_directories=],
individual files are numbered from ([=meta/num_directories=] + 1) to ([=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=]),
and special entries are numbered from ([=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=] + 1)
to ([=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=] + [=meta/num_specials=]).
There MUST be at least one entry other than the root directory,
i.e. [=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=] + [=meta/num_specials=] ≥ 1.
A parent of the entry index |k| in [=meta/entry_parents=] MUST be a directory less than |k|,
i.e. in [0, min(|k| - 1, [=meta/num_directories=])], so that directories can't form a cycle.
The parent of the root directory (entry 0) and the entry 1 is always fixed to None and the root directory,
so they are omitted.

Issue: Whether a segment without any entry other than the root directory should be allowed,
e.g. to update only the archive-wide optional metadata or the [=entry extra=] of the root directory.

File parameters (<dfn>file_param</dfn>) occur for each file (but not directory), and consist of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>file_dictionary</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/num_dictionaries=])
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>file_offset</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/previous_metadata_offset=])
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

[=meta/file_dictionary=] is the dictionary used by the block at [=meta/file_offset=]
as a <em>1-based</em> index, or zero if no dictionary is used.
Therefore [=meta/file_dictionary=] = |k| refers to the block at [=meta/dictionary_offsets=][|k| - 1],
and [=meta/num_dictionaries=] is the maximum possible value for it.
A relative offset to the corresponding [=leaf block=], which starts the file's [=parcel=],
is recorded as [=meta/file_offset=], which can't be zero.
Multiple files MAY refer to the same leaf block, in which case they have the same content.

<div class=note>
  This is how an archive stores files with the same content only once,
  including files that were hard links to each other ([[#link-groups]]).
  It costs only a [=meta/file_param=] and an [=meta/entry=] per file.
</div>

TODO: An output index when the formula block at [=meta/file_offset=] has multiple outputs.

Entry structure (<dfn>entry</dfn>) then occurs for each directory, file and special entry, in this order, and consists of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entry_name</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*] ≠ 0
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entry_name_sentinel</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=] = 0
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>original_size</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>[=If=] this entry is for a file
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>special_kind</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>[=If=] this entry is for a special entry
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>link_target</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*] ≠ 0
      <td>[=If=] this entry is for a special entry and [=meta/special_kind=] = 0
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>link_target_sentinel</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=] = 0
      <td>[=If=] this entry is for a special entry and [=meta/special_kind=] = 0
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>device_major</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>[=If=] this entry is for a special entry and [=meta/special_kind=] is 2 or 3
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>device_minor</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>[=If=] this entry is for a special entry and [=meta/special_kind=] is 2 or 3
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>special_data</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>[=If=] this entry is for a special entry and [=meta/special_kind=] > 3
  </tbody>
</table>

Each entry is named according to [=meta/entry_name=].
No name can contain a zero byte, and there are two allowed encodings:

* WTF-8 encoding [[WTF-8]], which is an extension to UTF-8 and losslessly encodes a lone surrogate pair in UTF-16; or
* Byte C1<sub>16</sub> followed by the raw byte sequence.

  <div class=note>
    Byte C1<sub>16</sub> was chosen because it can't occur in WTF-8 and any other known extension to UTF-8.
  </div>

Conforming compressors are strongly RECOMMENDED to use the WTF-8 encoding,
and SHOULD only use the raw byte sequence as a round-trip measure.

A special entry represents a node other than a file or a directory, as determined by [=meta/special_kind=]:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>[=meta/special_kind=]
      <th>Node
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>0
      <td>[=Link node=], with [=meta/link_target=] as its target
    <tr>
      <td>1
      <td>[=Special node=] of the kind "`fifo`"
    <tr>
      <td>2
      <td>[=Special node=] of the kind "`char-device`", with [=meta/device_major=] and [=meta/device_minor=] as its device number
    <tr>
      <td>3
      <td>[=Special node=] of the kind "`block-device`", with [=meta/device_major=] and [=meta/device_minor=] as its device number
    <tr>
      <td>4 or greater
      <td>[=Unknown node=], reserved for future versions of this specification
  </tbody>
</table>

[=meta/link_target=] uses the same encodings as [=meta/entry_name=].
[=meta/special_data=] is not interpreted by this version of this specification;
its length is determined by the beginning of the next entry in [=meta/entry_offsets=],
or [=meta/entries_end=] for the last entry.

<div class=note>
  Special entries are numbered separately from files, as directories are,
  so that [=meta/file_params=] remain directly indexed by entry numbers.
  A link target is stored in the entry rather than in the [[#optional-metadata|optional metadata]],
  because it defines the node itself and can't be ignored.
  Since the metadata block is compressed as a whole, similar targets compress well.

  Unknown kinds are not an [=invalid archive=] failure,
  so that a decompressor can still extract the rest of an archive using a kind defined in the future.
  Every special entry can be skipped without knowing its kind, thanks to [=meta/entry_offsets=].
</div>

The [=meta/entries=] array is the only part that has the unspecified number of bytes,
which is therefore available as [=meta/entries_size=] beforehand.
Any other field can be randomly accessed after reading the initial set of [=leb128=] fields.

As an exception to the relative offsets above, [=meta/entry_offsets=] are measured from [=meta/entries_start=].
The entry 1 always starts at [=meta/entries_start=], so its offset is omitted,
and [=meta/entry_offsets=][|k| - 2] is the offset to the beginning of the entry |k| for each |k| ≥ 2.
They MUST agree with the actual positions of [=meta/entries=].

[=meta/optional_metadata=] is described in Optional metadata ([[#optional-metadata]]).

</div>

<h5 id=entry-names-from-zip>Entry names from ZIP</h5>

<p><em>This section is non-normative.</em></p>

<p>A ZIP archive [[APPNOTE]] stores the path of each entry as a byte sequence in the local and central headers,
with components separated by `/`.
Its encoding is not always known from the header alone,
and several extra fields carry the Unicode version of the same path.
A compressor converting a ZIP archive can determine the encoding of each component from the following sources,
where earlier sources are preferred:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Extra field 0x7075 (Info-ZIP Unicode Path) of version 1 [[INFOZIP-EXTRAFLD]],
  whose `NameCRC32` matches the CRC-32 of the path in the header.
  The field holds the path in UTF-8.
  A field with a mismatching CRC-32 is ignored as specified by [[APPNOTE]],
  as it may have been left over by a tool that changed the path without updating the field.
  <li>The path in the header, if bit 11 (language encoding flag) of the general purpose bit flag is set.
  The path is in UTF-8.
  <li>Extra field 0x554e (Xceed Unicode), which holds the path in UTF-16LE.
  It is converted to WTF-8, which preserves any lone surrogate.
  <li>Extra field 0x5a4c (ZipArchive code page),
  which holds the path encoded in the code page given in the same field.
  If the field only has the code page, the path in the header is decoded with it instead.
  <li>Extra field 0xe57a (ALZip code page), which gives the code page of the path in the header.
  <li>Otherwise, the encoding of the path in the header is not specified.
  It is traditionally IBM code page 437, but often the system code page of the machine that made the archive,
  so the compressor has to guess the encoding.
  If it can't, the raw byte sequence (prefixed with C1<sub>16</sub>) is used for each component.
</ol>

<div class=note>
  <p>When the name comes from any source other than the last one,
  the original bytes of a path not in UTF-8 are not preserved.
  They only reflect how the ZIP container encodes the name,
  while the name of the file itself is the decoded Unicode string.</p>
</div>


<h4 id=content-block>
Content blocks</h4>

Any block other than spacer, size hint, metadata, [[#mirror-block|mirror]] and [=ignored blocks=]
is a <dfn>content block</dfn> which has to be described by a subsequent [[#metadata-block|metadata block]].
A metadata block and all content blocks between the previous metadata block (or the beginning of the archive if none)
and that metadata block form a <dfn>segment</dfn>.
As an exception, an archive without a metadata block ([[#unencrypted-archive]]) forms a single segment with no metadata block.
Each segment MUST be self-contained:
every reference from a block in a segment, whether by an index or by an offset, MUST point to a block in the same segment.

Content blocks are classified as follows:

* A <dfn>leaf block</dfn> represents the content of one or more files by itself.
    Leaf [[#data-block|data blocks]] and [[#formula-block|formula blocks]] are leaf blocks.
* A <dfn>non-leaf block</dfn> doesn't represent any file by itself,
    and only supplies data referenced by one or more formula blocks.
    Non-leaf [[#data-block|data blocks]] are non-leaf blocks.
* An <dfn>auxiliary block</dfn> supplies data used to decode other blocks.
    [[#dictionary-block|Dictionary blocks]], [[#module-block|module blocks]] and [[#resource-block|resource blocks]]
    are auxiliary blocks.

Dictionary, module and resource blocks are respectively indexed in the order of appearance within the segment.
Two conventions are used for these indices:

* Fields in content blocks use <em>0-based</em> indices,
    because the presence of a reference is signalled separately (e.g. by the block type).
* Fields in the metadata block that may indicate the absence of a reference use <em>1-based</em> indices,
    where zero denotes the absence.

<h5 id=block-order>
Block order</h5>

Blocks in a segment MUST be ordered so that a formula block is resolved by blocks preceding it
and all data required to evaluate it follows it:

* A dictionary block MUST precede any block using it.
* A module or resource block MUST precede any formula block referencing it.
* Every non-leaf block MUST be referenced by at least one formula block,
    and MUST follow all formula blocks referencing it.
* Leaf blocks are otherwise not constrained and MAY be interleaved with non-leaf blocks.

<div class=note>
  These rules make every reference from a formula block to a non-leaf block a forward reference.
  Therefore, when a non-leaf block is read in a streaming fashion,
  all formula blocks referencing it have already been read,
  and the decompressor knows exactly when the block can be discarded.
  Likewise, the decompressor never has to retain a formula block after all non-leaf blocks it references have been read.
</div>

A leaf block and all subsequent content blocks up to the next leaf block or the end of the segment
form a <dfn>parcel</dfn>.
When no leaf block is interleaved with non-leaf blocks,
a parcel consists of a leaf block, the non-leaf blocks it references if it is a formula block,
and possibly auxiliary blocks.
In that case a decompressor can read a single file by reading its parcel and the auxiliary blocks it uses,
and a streaming decompressor never has to retain a formula block beyond its parcel.

<figure id=fig-segments>
  <pre class=include-raw>path: figures/segments.svg</pre>
  <figcaption>
    An archive with two segments, each ending with the metadata block describing it,
    and four [=parcels=], each starting with a leaf block.
    Arrows denote references from formula blocks to non-leaf blocks (solid) and to a module block (dashed).
    The module block precedes the first leaf block in its segment, so it belongs to no parcel.
    Dictionary, resource and spacer blocks are omitted,
    and no leaf block is interleaved with non-leaf blocks.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<h5 id=data-block>
Data block</h5>

Blocks 20<sub>16</sub> through 37<sub>16</sub> and 40<sub>16</sub> through 4B<sub>16</sub> are **data blocks**.
A data block is a **compressed block** if its [=compression_method=] is not None,
and an **uncompressed block** otherwise.
Data blocks are parametrized by following variables:

<div dfn-for=dblock>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Block Types
      <th><dfn>leaf</dfn>
      <th><dfn>has_dictionary</dfn>
      <th>[=compression_method=]
      <th>[=checksum_method=]
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>20<sub>16</sub> — 23<sub>16</sub>
      <td>True
      <td>False
      <td>None
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>24<sub>16</sub> — 27<sub>16</sub>
      <td>True
      <td>False
      <td>None
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
    <tr>
      <td>28<sub>16</sub> — 2B<sub>16</sub>
      <td>True
      <td>False
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>2C<sub>16</sub> — 2F<sub>16</sub>
      <td>True
      <td>False
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
    <tr>
      <td>30<sub>16</sub> — 33<sub>16</sub>
      <td>True
      <td>True
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>34<sub>16</sub> — 37<sub>16</sub>
      <td>True
      <td>True
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
    <tr>
      <td>40<sub>16</sub> — 43<sub>16</sub>
      <td>False
      <td>False
      <td>None
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
    <tr>
      <td>44<sub>16</sub> — 47<sub>16</sub>
      <td>False
      <td>False
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
    <tr>
      <td>48<sub>16</sub> — 4B<sub>16</sub>
      <td>False
      <td>True
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class=note>
  Alternatively, the block type can be interpreted as a binary number
  <code>001|kk|&thinsp;|c||ss|</code> for a leaf block or <code>0100&thinsp;|kk||ss|</code> for a non-leaf block, where:

    * |kk| indicates the compression method and dictionary usage:
        0 for None, 1 for [[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]] without dictionary,
        2 for [[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]] with dictionary.
        3 is reserved for future compression methods.
    * |c| indicates the checksum method: 0 for None, 1 for [[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]].
        A non-leaf block always uses [[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]].
    * |ss| indicates the size of the size field: 0 for [=u8=], 1 for [=u16le=], 2 for [=u32le=], 3 for [=u64le=].
</div>

Its data consists of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>dictionary_index</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>[=If=] [=dblock/has_dictionary=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>compressed_data</dfn>
      <td>[=block/compressed_data=]
      <td>[=If=] [=compression_method=] is not None
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>uncompressed_data</dfn>
      <td>[=block/uncompressed_data=]
      <td>[=If=] [=compression_method=] is None
  </tbody>
</table>

If a dictionary is in use, its <em>0-based</em> index is specified as [=dblock/dictionary_index=].
This field is intended for linear scanning,
and the metadata block contains all necessary information for random access.
[=dblock/compressed_data=] and [=dblock/uncompressed_data=] use the Shared external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]])
with [=compression_method=] and [=checksum_method=] determined as above.
[=dblock/uncompressed_data=] represents a raw, uninterpreted byte sequence.

An uncompressed block MUST be used when given data is barely compressible and can't be represented in terms of compressed blocks.

<div class=note>
  This is mandatory because each Zstandard block incurs 3 bytes of overhead
  and [=cdata/original_size_delta=] can't be negative.
</div>

A compressed block is a single unit of decompression:
reading any part of its content requires decompressing it from the beginning.
Content larger than a desired unit of random access is split into multiple blocks:

* In general, a file is represented by a [[#formula-block|formula block]] concatenating multiple non-leaf blocks,
    which typically form a single [=parcel=] together.
    The formula block then acts as an index for random access within the parcel.
* In an archive without a metadata block ([[#unencrypted-archive]]),
    the file is simply represented by multiple leaf blocks.

<div class=note>
  The checksum is mandatory for non-leaf blocks.
  The output checksum of a formula block covers its entire output,
  so a decompressor that only reads a part of the output can't verify it without reading the whole output.
  Instead it can verify the checksums of non-leaf blocks it has actually read.
  The checksum also lets damage to a non-leaf block be detected before the block is used by a module application,
  so that the damage is always an [=invalid archive=] failure rather than a [=resource failure=],
  as for [=formula/formula_data=] ([[#formula-block]]).
</div>

Unless explicitly requested otherwise, conforming compressors SHOULD use a [=checksum_method=] other than None
for every leaf block.

<div class=note>
  The checksum is optional for leaf blocks to allow the following:

    * An uncompressed leaf block without a checksum holds the file content verbatim and nothing derived from it,
        so the file can be memory-mapped from the archive and modified in place.
    * A compressed leaf block without a checksum can be made from an existing Zstandard frame by copying its Zstandard blocks,
        without decompressing them to compute the checksum over the decoded data.

  Neither is a concern for long-term preservation, where the [=archival profile=] requires the checksum.
</div>

</div>

<h5 id=dictionary-block>
Dictionary block</h5>

Blocks 50<sub>16</sub> through 57<sub>16</sub> are **dictionary blocks**.
Their data uses the Shared external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]]),
with [[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]] as [=compression_method=]
and [[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]] as [=checksum_method=].
Dictionary blocks are parametrized by following variables:

<div dfn-for=dict>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Block Types
      <th><dfn>has_entropy_tables</dfn>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>50<sub>16</sub> — 53<sub>16</sub>
      <td>False
    <tr>
      <td>54<sub>16</sub> — 57<sub>16</sub>
      <td>True
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class=note>
  Alternatively, the block type can be interpreted as a binary number <code>0101&thinsp;0|t||ss|</code> where:

    * |t| indicates the presence of entropy tables: 0 for absent, 1 for present.
    * |ss| indicates the size of the size field: 0 for [=u8=], 1 for [=u16le=], 2 for [=u32le=], 3 for [=u64le=].
</div>

The internal structure after decompression is as follows:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entropy_tables</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>[=If=] [=dict/has_entropy_tables=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>repeat_offsets</dfn>
      <td>[=u32le=][3]
      <td>[=If=] [=dict/has_entropy_tables=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>content</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

The decoded data of a dictionary block is a [=dictionary=] for blocks using it,
and corresponds to a Zstandard dictionary ([[RFC8878]], Section 5) without `Magic_Number` and `Dictionary_ID`:

*   If [=dict/has_entropy_tables=] is true, [=dict/entropy_tables=] is the `Entropy_Tables` structure,
    whose length is determined by parsing it, and [=dict/repeat_offsets=] are the three `Repeat_Offsets` in order.
    Each repeat offset MUST be in [1, length of [=dict/content=]].
*   Otherwise, it is a raw content dictionary:
    the entropy tables start empty and the repeat offsets start at their initial values (1, 4, 8),
    even when [=dict/content=] begins with the dictionary `Magic_Number`.

In both cases, [=dict/content=] is the `Content` of the dictionary,
which is the only part of the dictionary that sequences can reference as preceding data.
[=dict/content=] MUST be at least 8 bytes long.

<div class=note>
  `Magic_Number` and `Dictionary_ID` are omitted because they are only meaningful for Zstandard frames
  and the framing is done by this format.
  An implementation using libzstd can prepend them (with an arbitrary nonzero `Dictionary_ID`)
  to load a dictionary with entropy tables.
  In that case it has to make sure that no sequence references the prepended bytes or [=dict/entropy_tables=],
  which some decompression paths of libzstd don't check.
</div>

<div class=note>
  Entropy tables can be trained independently of the content (e.g. `ZDICT_finalizeDictionary`)
  and mainly benefit small blocks:
  in measurements with per-file blocks of 1–20 KB files, they reduced the size by 0.4–26%,
  while the benefit vanished for blocks of 64 KB or more.
  Fast strategies of libzstd (e.g. levels 1–3 of 1.5.7) reuse the dictionary's entropy tables for every block
  even when the block's own tables would be smaller.
  When the repeat offsets of the dictionary are the initial values,
  a compressor can avoid this by also compressing a block with the same [=dict/content=] as a raw content dictionary
  and choosing the smaller result,
  since a block compressed that way never refers to the entropy tables of the dictionary.
</div>

</div>

<h5 id=module-block>
Module block</h5>

Blocks 60<sub>16</sub> through 63<sub>16</sub> are **module blocks**.
Their data uses the Shared external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]]),
with [[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]] as [=compression_method=] 
and [[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]] as [=checksum_method=].

<div class=note>
  Alternatively, the block type can be interpreted as a binary number <code>0110&thinsp;00|ss|</code> where:

    * |ss| indicates the size of the size field: 0 for [=u8=], 1 for [=u16le=], 2 for [=u32le=], 3 for [=u64le=].
</div>

The internal structure after decompression is as follows:

<div dfn-for=module>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>wasm_module</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

TODO: The WebAssembly profile, the module interface and the identification by a hash.

</div>

<h5 id=resource-block>
Resource block</h5>

Blocks 64<sub>16</sub> through 67<sub>16</sub> are **resource blocks**.
Their data uses the Shared external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]]),
with [[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]] as [=compression_method=] 
and [[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]] as [=checksum_method=].

<div class=note>
  Alternatively, the block type can be interpreted as a binary number <code>0110&thinsp;01|ss|</code> where:

    * |ss| indicates the size of the size field: 0 for [=u8=], 1 for [=u16le=], 2 for [=u32le=], 3 for [=u64le=].
</div>

The internal structure after decompression is an uninterpreted byte sequence,
which is supplied to modules by formula blocks referencing it ([[#formula-data]], [[#module-execution]]).

<h5 id=formula-block>
Formula block</h5>

Blocks 70<sub>16</sub> through 77<sub>16</sub> are **formula blocks**.
Formula blocks are parametrized by following variables:

<div dfn-for=formula>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Block Types
      <th><dfn>has_dictionary</dfn>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>70<sub>16</sub> — 73<sub>16</sub>
      <td>False
    <tr>
      <td>74<sub>16</sub> — 77<sub>16</sub>
      <td>True
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class=note>
  Alternatively, the block type can be interpreted as a binary number <code>0111&thinsp;0|d||ss|</code> where:

    * |d| indicates dictionary usage: 0 for no dictionary, 1 for dictionary present.
    * |ss| indicates the size of the size field: 0 for [=u8=], 1 for [=u16le=], 2 for [=u32le=], 3 for [=u64le=].
</div>

Its data consists of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>dictionary_index</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>[=If=] [=formula/has_dictionary=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>formula_data</dfn>
      <td>[=block/compressed_data=]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

If a dictionary is in use, its <em>0-based</em> index is specified as [=formula/dictionary_index=], as in data blocks.
[=formula/formula_data=] uses the Shared compressed external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]])
with [[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]] as [=compression_method=]
and [[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]] as [=checksum_method=].

<div class=note>
  Formula blocks always use Zstandard, which can still store data verbatim with raw Zstandard blocks.
  Many small formula blocks are expected to share a dictionary to compensate for the lack of shared compression context.
</div>

<div class=note>
  The checksum of [=formula/formula_data=] is mandatory and distinct from the optional [=output checksums=]
  declared inside [=formula/formula_data=] ([[#formula-evaluation]]).
  [=formula/formula_data=] is always decoded in its entirety before evaluation,
  so its checksum can be verified even by a decompressor reading only a part of the output.
  It lets a damaged formula block be detected by reading the block alone and before evaluating anything,
  so that a damaged formula block is always an [=invalid archive=] failure
  rather than a [=resource failure=] caused by damaged resource declarations,
  and is distinguished from damage to the non-leaf blocks it references.
  An output checksum, on the other hand, also verifies the evaluation including module applications,
  but can only be verified after computing the entire output.
</div>

Unless explicitly requested otherwise, conforming compressors SHOULD declare an [=output checksum=]
for each output computed by an [[#insn-apply|Apply instruction]],
so that the module application is also verified.
Other outputs are already covered by the checksums of the non-leaf blocks they are computed from.

A formula block describes the content of a file as an expression over non-leaf blocks following it,
evaluated with modules and resources preceding it.
The internal structure of [=formula/formula_data=] is described in Formula data ([[#formula-data]]).

</div>

<h4 id=mirror-block>
Mirror block</h4>

A **mirror block** holds a verbatim copy of another block, called its <dfn for=mirror>target block</dfn>,
so that the target block can be restored when it is damaged.
Mirror blocks are only defined for block types whose damage affects more than a single [=parcel=]
and can be detected by reading the block alone, i.e. that have a checksum:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Block Types
      <th>Target Block Types
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>C10<sub>16</sub> — C17<sub>16</sub>
      <td>10<sub>16</sub> — 17<sub>16</sub> ([[#metadata-block]])
    <tr>
      <td>C50<sub>16</sub> — C57<sub>16</sub>
      <td>50<sub>16</sub> — 57<sub>16</sub> ([[#dictionary-block]])
    <tr>
      <td>C60<sub>16</sub> — C63<sub>16</sub>
      <td>60<sub>16</sub> — 63<sub>16</sub> ([[#module-block]])
    <tr>
      <td>C64<sub>16</sub> — C67<sub>16</sub>
      <td>64<sub>16</sub> — 67<sub>16</sub> ([[#resource-block]])
    <tr>
      <td>C70<sub>16</sub> — C77<sub>16</sub>
      <td>70<sub>16</sub> — 77<sub>16</sub> ([[#formula-block]])
  </tbody>
</table>

As with any other block, the [=block/size=] field of a mirror block is determined by its own [=block/type=]
and counts the bytes of its own [=block/data=].
Since a mirror block is longer than its target block, its [=block/size=] field may have to be wider.
Therefore a mirror block is either:

* <dfn for=mirror>Unwidened</dfn>, where the [=block/size=] fields of the mirror block and its target block have the same width,
    and its [=mirror/target type=] is its [=block/type=] minus C00<sub>16</sub>; or
* <dfn for=mirror>Widened</dfn>, where the [=block/size=] field of the mirror block is one step wider than that of its target block,
    and its [=mirror/target type=] is its [=block/type=] minus C01<sub>16</sub>.
    Its [=block/type=] % 4 MUST NOT be 0.

The [=mirror/target block=] MUST be of the <dfn for=mirror>target type</dfn>,
which MUST be a block type listed above for the mirror block's [=block/type=].
Whether a mirror block is widened is signalled by [=mirror/target_offset=].

<div class=note>
  For example, a mirror block of type C13<sub>16</sub> mirrors a metadata block of type 13<sub>16</sub>,
  which is uncompressed and has a [=u64le=] [=block/size=] field, if unwidened,
  or a metadata block of type 12<sub>16</sub> with a [=u32le=] [=block/size=] field if widened.
  Since C00<sub>16</sub> % 4 = 0, both stay in the same row of the table above.
</div>

Its data consists of the following:

<div dfn-for=mirror>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>mirror_magic</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][8] = [=const/MIRROR_MAGIC=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>archive_offset</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] = [=block/block_start=] - [=archive/archive_start=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>target_offset</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] ≠ 0
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>target_data</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>checksum_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>mirror_checksum</dfn>
      <td>[=u64le=]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

[=mirror/mirror_magic=] is a fixed byte sequence that allows mirror blocks to be found by scanning
when the surrounding structure, such as the header, the footer or the metadata chain, is damaged.

[=mirror/archive_offset=] is the offset from [=archive/archive_start=] to the [=block/block_start=] of the mirror block.

<div class=note>
  [=mirror/archive_offset=] distinguishes mirror blocks of this archive from those of another archive stored inside it,
  which can be found by scanning and verify by themselves just like the mirror blocks of this archive.
  Such a mirror block may point to bytes that are not its target block in this archive,
  for example when the inner archive is split into multiple blocks.
  Since the inner archive is stored after [=archive/archive_start=] of this archive,
  the start of the archive implied by its [=mirror/archive_offset=] is always greater.
</div>

[=mirror/target_offset=] is the offset from the [=block/block_start=] of the [=mirror/target block=]
to the [=block/block_start=] of the mirror block, plus 1 if the mirror block is [=mirror/widened=].
Since the offset between any two blocks is even, [=mirror/target_offset=] % 2 tells whether the mirror block is widened.
The target block always precedes the mirror block.

[=mirror/target_data=] is a copy of the [=block/data=] of the target block.
The bytes of the target block, from its [=block/block_start=] to its [=block/block_end=],
MUST be equal to the concatenation of the following:

1. The shortest [=leb128=] encoding of the [=mirror/target type=];
2. The length of [=mirror/target_data=], encoded as the [=block/size=] field for the [=mirror/target type=];
3. [=mirror/target_data=].

The [=block/padding=] of the target block, if any, is not compared, and is restored as a zero byte.
The [=block/type=] of the mirror block itself is 2 bytes long, as every block type uses the shortest [=leb128=] encoding.

[=mirror/mirror_checksum=] is the XXH64 [[XXHASH]] hash with the seed zero
of all bytes from the [=block/block_start=] of the mirror block to [=mirror/checksum_start=],
including its [=block/type=] and [=block/size=] fields.

</div>

<div class=note>
  Unlike other checksums in this specification, [=mirror/mirror_checksum=] is computed over the stored bytes,
  so that the copy can be verified without decoding it and the mirror block can be verified when found by scanning.
  A decompressor normally uses a mirror block only after the checksum of its target block fails,
  while a verifier can also compare the copy with the target block directly.
</div>

<div class=note>
  A mirror block is a copy of bytes, not a block to be interpreted at its own position.
  This is necessary because relative offsets in a metadata block are measured from the metadata block itself.
</div>

A mirror block SHOULD be [=mirror/unwidened=] unless its [=block/data=] doesn't fit in the [=block/size=] field for the target type.

<div class=note>
  The data of a mirror block is at least 18 bytes longer than that of its target block,
  so this is only relevant when the target block is close to the limit of its [=block/size=] field.
  A single step is always sufficient, since the next width can hold far more than the additional bytes.
  The width of the target block's [=block/size=] field can't be inferred from the size of [=mirror/target_data=],
  because a block may use a wider [=block/size=] field than necessary.
</div>

<div class=note>
  A block with a [=u64le=] [=block/size=] field can't be mirrored when its data is too close to 2<sup>64</sup> bytes,
  since its mirror block can't be widened.
  This is not a practical concern, because such a block is far larger than 2<sup>63</sup> bytes,
  which is beyond what most systems with signed 64-bit file offsets can store in a single file.
</div>

<div class=note>
  To find mirror blocks by scanning, a decoder can look for [=const/MIRROR_MAGIC=]
  and try each of four possible widths of the [=block/size=] field preceding it.
  The candidate is correct when the 2 bytes before the [=block/size=] field encode a mirror block type with the matching width
  and [=mirror/mirror_checksum=] verifies.
  The candidate is then discarded unless its [=block/block_start=] minus [=mirror/archive_offset=]
  equals [=archive/archive_start=], because it belongs to another archive stored inside this archive otherwise.
  If [=archive/archive_start=] can't be determined from [=archive/header_magic=] or [=archive/size_hint=],
  the smallest value implied by verified candidates can be used instead.
</div>

A mirror block doesn't belong to any [=segment=] and MAY occur anywhere in [=archive/blocks=],
including after the last metadata block, subject only to the requirement that its target block precedes it.
A single target block MAY have multiple mirror blocks.
Mirror blocks don't affect the result of [=interpret an archive|interpreting an archive=],
except that an archive violating the requirements above is an [=invalid archive=].

<div class=note>
  By [[#partial-access]], a decompressor not reading mirror blocks is not required to verify them.
</div>

A mirror block and its target block are expected to be damaged together when they are close to each other.
Conforming compressors SHOULD separate the end of the target block and the [=block/block_start=] of its mirror block
by at least 4096 bytes, so that they never share an aligned 4 KiB unit of the underlying storage,
and SHOULD separate them by at least 4 MiB (4,194,304 bytes) when possible.

Issue: The recommended distances are tentative, pending the measurement of burst lengths of latent sector errors
(`recovery-design.md` §8, R1).

<div class=note>
  Dictionary, module and formula blocks typically occur early in their segments,
  so their mirror blocks can be placed near the end of the segment.
  A metadata block, on the other hand, only becomes available at the end of its segment,
  so its mirror block can be placed after a sufficient amount of the next segment has been written.
  The mirror block of the last metadata block can't be distant from it unless the compressor delays the last segment,
  and closing segments periodically bounds the loss in that case.
</div>

<div class=note>
  Restoring damaged blocks from mirror blocks is a process separate from [=interpret an archive|interpreting an archive=]:
  it takes a damaged byte sequence and replaces each target block whose bytes differ from a verified mirror block
  with the bytes reconstructed from the mirror block,
  locating the target block with [=mirror/target_offset=] alone.
</div>

Issue: The repair process is to be specified in a separate section (`recovery-design.md` §5.6).

<h4 id=reserved-blocks>
Reserved blocks</h4>

A block type is <dfn>critical</dfn> if its hexadecimal representation starts with 0<sub>16</sub> through 9<sub>16</sub>,
and <dfn>non-critical</dfn> if it starts with A<sub>16</sub> through F<sub>16</sub>.
A block whose type is not defined by this specification:

* Results in an [=unsupported failure=] ([[#interpretation]]) if its type is [=critical=];
* Is an <dfn>ignored block</dfn> otherwise.
    Like a mirror block, an ignored block doesn't belong to any [=segment=], MAY occur anywhere in [=archive/blocks=]
    and doesn't affect the result of [=interpret an archive|interpreting an archive=].

<div class=note>
  Every block type defined by this specification is [=critical=] except for [[#mirror-block|mirror blocks]],
  which a decompressor not using them can skip.
  Future versions of this specification will use a critical type for a block
  without which an archive can't be correctly interpreted,
  e.g. a [[#data-block|data block]] with a new compression method in 38<sub>16</sub> through 3F<sub>16</sub>,
  and a non-critical type for a block that can be safely skipped, e.g. recovery data.
  The block-like structures below also have critical types, so they are never skipped by mistake.
</div>

Blocks whose type's hexadecimal representation is at least 2 digits long
and starts with D<sub>16</sub> (e.g. D0<sub>16</sub> through DF<sub>16</sub>, D00<sub>16</sub> through DFF<sub>16</sub>, …)
are <dfn>reserved for development environments</dfn>.
This specification doesn't assign any meanings to these blocks.
These blocks MUST be written or read only with specific instructions to enable them.

Blocks which type's hexadecimal representation is at least 2 digits long
and starts with E<sub>16</sub> (e.g. E0<sub>16</sub> through EF<sub>16</sub>, E00<sub>16</sub> through EFF<sub>16</sub>, …)
are <dfn>reserved for experiments</dfn>.
This specification doesn't assign any meanings to those blocks.

<div class=note>
  Blocks reserved for development environments and experiments are [=non-critical=],
  so a decompressor not enabling them ignores them.
  An implementation enabling an experiment may treat its blocks as critical,
  since the experiment is expected to move to a block type defined by this specification once finished.
</div>

The following block types are defined as invalid due to the use as block-like structures.
The valid archive MUST NOT include such block types as a proper block.

* Block 278<sub>16</sub> ([=const/HEADER_TYPE=]) is used for the unencrypted archive header.
* Block 98&thinsp;68EB&thinsp;D5FD<sub>16</sub> ([=const/FOOTER_TYPE=]) is used for the archive footer.
* Block 15D&thinsp;14FC&thinsp;E5F9<sub>16</sub> ([=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_TYPE=]) is used for the encrypted archive header.

<h3 id=archive>
Archive</h3>

<h4 id=unencrypted-archive>
Unencrypted Archive</h4>

An **unencrypted archive** consists of a header, one or more blocks and a footer.

<div dfn-for=archive>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>header_magic</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][6] = [=const/HEADER_MAGIC=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>archive_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>size_hint_block</dfn>
      <td>[[#size-hint-block|size_hint_block]]
      <td>[=cond/Optional=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>blocks</dfn>
      <td>[[#block|block]][*]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>archive_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>footer_magic</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][6] = [=const/FOOTER_MAGIC=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>footer_size</dfn>
      <td>[=u16le=] = [=archive/footer_end=] - [=archive/footer_start=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>footer_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>size_hint</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] = [=archive/archive_end=] - [=archive/archive_start=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>metadata_backward_offset</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>footer_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>footer_size_copy</dfn>
      <td>[=u16le=] = [=archive/footer_size=]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

[=archive/header_magic=] is a fixed 6-byte sequence to identify the file as an unencrypted Aqueous archive.
This header also marks the beginning of the archive, when the archive doesn't start at the beginning of the file.

[=archive/footer_magic=] is a fixed 6-byte sequence to mark the beginning of the footer.
The footer also marks the end of the archive, when the archive doesn't end at the end of the file.
The offset between [=archive/header_magic=] and [=archive/footer_magic=] is recorded as [=archive/size_hint=],
which MAY be also replicated as [=archive/size_hint_block=] if random access is desired.

The footer size, which is the size of all fields after (but not including) [=archive/footer_magic=],
is recorded both in [=archive/footer_size=] and in [=archive/footer_size_copy=].
Implementations are therefore expected to first identify [=archive/header_magic=] and skip to the end of the file
to read [=archive/footer_size_copy=] and eventually [=archive/footer_magic=] to read the footer.

<div class=note>
  [=archive/footer_size=] and [=archive/footer_size_copy=] are [=u16le=] instead of [=leb128=].
  Therefore the footer size is limited to 65,535 bytes,
  and a single read of 65,543 bytes (including [=archive/footer_magic=] and [=archive/footer_size_copy=]) is
  sufficient to guarantee that the entire footer is read.
</div>

[=archive/metadata_backward_offset=] is the offset from [=archive/footer_start=] to the beginning of last metadata block,
just like [=meta/previous_metadata_offset=] is the offset from the beginning of the current metadata block to the previous one.
It MUST be 0 if and only if there is no metadata block,
so that a decompressor can tell such archives without scanning [=archive/blocks=].

[=archive/blocks=] are logically divided into [=segments=],
each consisting of [=content blocks=] and a single [[#metadata-block|metadata block]] describing them,
with optional spacer, [[#mirror-block|mirror]] and [=ignored blocks=] in between.
Each segment can be independently processed and decompressed in the reading order.
There SHOULD NOT be multiple non-directory [=meta/entry|entries=] with the same path across metadata blocks,
and if there are, only the last one is considered valid ([[#segment-merging]]).

Alternatively, [=archive/blocks=] can have no metadata block.
Such archive decompresses into a single unnamed file,
whose content is the concatenation of the contents of all [=leaf blocks=] in the order of appearance.
It decompresses into an empty file if there is no leaf block.

<div class=note>
  This allows a single file of unknown size to be compressed in a streaming fashion,
  by writing each unit of random access as a separate leaf block, and thus a separate [=parcel=], as soon as it is compressed.
  It is analogous to a gzip file that doesn't need to contain the name of the decompressed file.
  Compressing multiple files in a streaming fashion is not supported:
  a file spanning multiple non-leaf blocks requires a [=parcel=] starting with a formula block,
  which declares the length of every slice of the non-leaf blocks before any of them.
</div>

</div>

<h4 id=encrypted-archive>
Encrypted Archive</h4>

An archive might have been **encrypted**,
in which case it consists of a single block of type [=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_TYPE=] followed by an encrypted binary payload.

<div dfn-for=envelope>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>encrypted_header_magic</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][8] = [=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_MAGIC=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>lg_work_factor</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=] ∈ [0C<sub>16</sub>, 3F<sub>16</sub>)
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>scrypt_ephemeral_secret</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][16]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>encrypted_file_key</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][32]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>age_header_mac</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][32]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>payload</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

All fields except for [=payload=] are used to reconstruct an AGE v1 textual file header [[AGE]] as follows:

<pre>
age-encryption.org/v1
-> scrypt <var>scrypt_ephemeral_secret_base64</var> <var>lg_work_factor_decimal</var>
<var>encrypted_file_key_base64</var>
--- <var>age_header_mac_base64</var>
</pre>

Where,

* |scrypt_ephemeral_secret_base64| is the base64 encoding of [=envelope/scrypt_ephemeral_secret=] without padding ([[RFC4648]], Section 4);
* |lg_work_factor_decimal| is the decimal representation of [=envelope/lg_work_factor=] without leading zeros;
* |encrypted_file_key_base64| is the base64 encoding of [=envelope/encrypted_file_key=] without padding;
* |age_header_mac_base64| is the base64 encoding of [=envelope/age_header_mac=] without padding.

Subsequent [=payload=] contains the file nonce and encrypted chunks and interpreted in accordance to the AGE v1 format.

The encrypted payload MUST decrypt into a valid unencrypted archive ([[#unencrypted-archive]]),
with the following additional constraints:

* [=archive/size_hint_block=] is no longer optional;
* No spacer block ([[#spacer-block]]) is allowed, in order to discourage an in-place modification of the encrypted archive;
* No mirror block ([[#mirror-block]]) is allowed;
* No trailing bytes are allowed after the archive.

Issue: Mirror blocks are disallowed because a damaged AGE chunk fails to authenticate as a whole,
and a standard AGE implementation stops at the first such chunk,
so a mirror inside the payload is not usable for repair.
Whether to allow them anyway, and how to place recovery data outside the payload, is open
(`recovery-design.md` §5.5).

</div>

<h3 id=optional-metadata>
Optional metadata</h3>

The [=meta/optional_metadata=] field of the metadata block ([[#metadata-block]]) holds the **optional metadata**, encoded in CBOR [[RFC8949]].
This part encodes any metadata that is not strictly necessary to read the file,
including associated dates and times, access control information and comments.

The optional metadata is described by the following CDDL [[RFC8610]] definitions:

<pre class=cddl>
aqr-opt-metadata = {
  aqr-archive-comment
  ? &(entry-extras: -2) => [* aqr-entry-extra / null]  ; Indexed by entry numbers, starting from the root directory
  * $$aqr-opt-metadata-member  ; For future extensions
}

$$aqr-opt-metadata-member = ()  ; No extension is defined by this specification

aqr-offset = uint .gt 0

aqr-entry-extra = {
  aqr-entry-timestamps
  aqr-entry-comment
  aqr-entry-link-group
  aqr-entry-windows
  aqr-entry-unix
  aqr-entry-zip
  * $$aqr-entry-extra-member  ; For future extensions
}

$$aqr-entry-extra-member = ()  ; No extension is defined by this specification

; TODO: other extras for round trip
</pre>

There MUST NOT be any following bytes after this CBOR item.
If [=meta/optional_metadata_size=] is 0, the optional metadata is an empty map `{}`.

The members of `aqr-entry-extra` are grouped by their purposes, and each group is described in the following sections.
Keys of `aqr-entry-extra` from -1 to -59 are platform-neutral.
Keys from -60 to -69 are specific to Windows, -70 to -79 to Unix, -80 to -89 to macOS, and -90 to -99 to other platforms.

<h4 id=timestamps>Timestamps</h4>

<p>The keys `crtime`, `ctime`, `mtime`, `atime` and `arctime` of <code>aqr-entry-extra</code>
record the timestamps that file systems and archive formats associate with a file, a directory or a link.
Each is an <code>aqr-time</code> described below, and is absent if the source doesn't have the corresponding timestamp.</p>

<pre class=cddl>
aqr-entry-timestamps = (
  ? &(crtime: -1) => aqr-time
  ? &(ctime: -2) => aqr-time
  ? &(mtime: -3) => aqr-time
  ? &(atime: -4) => aqr-time
  ? &(arctime: -5) => aqr-time
)
</pre>

<dl>
<dt>`crtime`</dt>
<dd><p>The creation time, also known as the birth time: when the file was created at its location.
It is not updated afterwards, but copying a file usually creates a new one with the time of copying.
Examples include the creation time of NTFS and FAT, the create date of HFS+ and APFS,
`i_crtime` of ext4 and `otime` of Btrfs,
which are exposed as `st_birthtime` on BSD and macOS and as `stx_btime` of `statx` on Linux.</p></dd>
<dt>`ctime`</dt>
<dd><p>The status change time:
when the file's metadata (e.g. permissions, ownership or link count) or its content was last changed.
It is the POSIX `st_ctime`, and the change time of NTFS and the attribute modification date of HFS+ are similar.
POSIX doesn't allow it to be set to an arbitrary time,
so an extracting decompressor usually can't restore it.</p></dd>
<dt>`mtime`</dt>
<dd><p>The modification time: when the file's content was last changed.
For a directory, it is when an entry was last added, removed or renamed in it.
This is the timestamp that virtually all file systems and archive formats have.</p></dd>
<dt>`atime`</dt>
<dd><p>The access time: when the file's content was last read.
Many systems update it lazily or not at all for performance
(e.g. `relatime` and `noatime` on Linux, and the access time updates of NTFS that can be disabled),
and FAT only keeps the date.
Reading a file for archiving can itself update it.</p></dd>
<dt>`arctime`</dt>
<dd><p>The backup time: when the file was last backed up, as recorded by backup software.
File systems don't update it by themselves.
An example is the backup date of HFS and HFS+, exposed as `ATTR_CMN_BKUPTIME` of `getattrlist` on macOS.
It is the time stored by the source, not the time when the archive itself was made.</p></dd>
</dl>

<div class=note>
  <p>The name "ctime" is ambiguous.
  Windows and formats originating from it use "C" for the creation time:
  `st_ctime` in the Microsoft C runtime, the `Ctime` of ZIP extra field 0x000a,
  and the `CTime` of 7z and RAR all go to `crtime`, not `ctime`.
  POSIX formats such as pax use it for the status change time, which goes to `ctime`.</p>
</div>

<p><code>aqr-time</code> represents a point in time or a local date and time,
together with a [=quantization interval=] and optional time zone information.
It is described by the following CDDL definitions,
where `etime` is the CDDL type name for the extended time defined in [[!RFC9581]]:</p>

<pre class=cddl>
aqr-time =
  $aqr-base-time /                          ; UTC timestamp, scaled and quantized
  [$aqr-base-time] /                        ; Local time in unknown time zone, encoded like UTC (*)
  [$aqr-instant, offset: aqr-tz-offset] /   ; UTC timestamp with time zone offset ($)
  etime /                                   ; Extended time defined in [[!RFC9581]]
  [etime]                                   ; Local time in unknown time zone, encoded like UTC

aqr-tz-offset = -48..56  ; In 15 minutes, positive east of UTC as in ISO 9660

$aqr-base-time  = #6.100(int)    ; 1d (FAT atime*), date as in [[RFC8943]]
$aqr-base-time /= $aqr-instant  ; Can be given a time zone offset

$aqr-instant  = #6.121(int)  ; 2s (FAT mtime*, exFAT atime$, ZIP*, CAB*)
$aqr-instant /= int          ; 1s (~ext3, HFS/HFS+, tar, ISO 9660$)
$aqr-instant /= #6.122(int)  ; 10ms (FAT ctime*, ISO 9660$, exFAT$)
$aqr-instant /= #6.123(int)  ; 1ms
$aqr-instant /= #6.124(int)  ; 1us (UDF)
$aqr-instant /= #6.125(int)  ; 100ns (NTFS, RAR*, 7z)
$aqr-instant /= #6.126(int)  ; 1ns (ext4, ZFS, Btrfs, APFS)
</pre>

<p>A base time value <code>$aqr-base-time</code> is an integer, either untagged or tagged as follows.
The integer multiplied by the scale is the number of seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z,
not counting leap seconds as in the CBOR tag 1 [[RFC8949]];
negative values denote the time before that epoch.
The tag also determines the quantization interval,
which is relative to the time value and specified in [[#etime-quantization-interval]].
`#6.100` is the number of days since 1970-01-01 [[RFC8943]],
and is the only base time value that is not an <code>&#36;aqr-instant</code>, i.e. it can't be given a time zone offset.</p>
<table class=data>
<thead>
<tr>
  <th>Encoding</th>
  <th>Quantization Interval (<abbr title="seconds">s</abbr>)</th>
  <th>Scale (<abbr title="seconds">s</abbr>)</th>
  <th>Examples</th>
</tr>
<tbody>
<tr>
  <td><code>#6.100(int)</code></td>
  <td>[0, 86400]</td>
  <td>86400</td>
  <td>FAT atime*</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>#6.121(int)</code></td>
  <td>[0, 2]</td>
  <td>2</td>
  <td>FAT mtime*, exFAT atime$, ZIP*, CAB*</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>int</code></td>
  <td>[0, 1]</td>
  <td>1</td>
  <td>ext3 and earlier, HFS/HFS+, tar, ISO 9660$ (directory records)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>#6.122(int)</code></td>
  <td>[0, 10<sup>-2</sup>]</td>
  <td>10<sup>-2</sup></td>
  <td>FAT ctime*, ISO 9660$ (volume descriptors), exFAT$</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>#6.123(int)</code></td>
  <td>[0, 10<sup>-3</sup>]</td>
  <td>10<sup>-3</sup></td>
  <td>-</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>#6.124(int)</code></td>
  <td>[0, 10<sup>-6</sup>]</td>
  <td>10<sup>-6</sup></td>
  <td>UDF</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>#6.125(int)</code></td>
  <td>[0, 10<sup>-7</sup>]</td>
  <td>10<sup>-7</sup></td>
  <td>NTFS, RAR*, 7z</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>#6.126(int)</code></td>
  <td>[0, 10<sup>-9</sup>]</td>
  <td>10<sup>-9</sup></td>
  <td>ext4, ZFS, Btrfs, APFS</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p>In the Examples column, formats marked with * store (or can store) a local time in an unknown time zone,
and formats marked with $ store a local time with its offset from UTC.</p>

<div class=note>
  <p>Tags 121 to 126 are the tags for enumerated alternatives 0 to 5 [[CBOR-NOTABLE-TAGS]],
  whose meanings are given by the schema in use rather than by the tags themselves.
  They are therefore meaningful only as <code>&#36;aqr-instant</code>,
  and are not shortcuts for the extended time in general.</p>
</div>

<div class=note>
  <p>Since a time zone offset is a multiple of 15 minutes, converting a local time to UTC keeps it a multiple of 2 seconds.
  A local date, however, doesn't stay a multiple of 1 day unless the offset is zero,
  so a date with a known time zone offset can only be given as an extended time
  (e.g. `1001({1: 1697641200, -14: 86400, -10: "+09:00"})` for 2023-10-19 in UTC+9).</p>
</div>

<p>Each alternative of <code>aqr-time</code> has the following meaning:</p>

<dl>
<dt><code>&#36;aqr-base-time</code></dt>
<dd>A point in time without any time zone information.</dd>
<dt><code>[&#36;aqr-base-time]</code></dt>
<dd>A local date and time in an unknown time zone, encoded as if it were UTC.
It does not identify a single point in time.</dd>
<dt><code>[&#36;aqr-instant, aqr-tz-offset]</code></dt>
<dd>A point in time, as in the bare <code>&#36;aqr-base-time</code>,
with the offset of the time zone where it was recorded, in 15-minute increments as in ISO 9660.
The offset is positive east of UTC: UTC+<var>x</var> hours is 4<var>x</var> and UTC&minus;<var>x</var> hours is &minus;4<var>x</var>.
The local time is the base time value plus 900 times the offset in seconds.</dd>
<dt><code>etime</code></dt>
<dd>An extended time [[RFC9581]] (tag 1001), for any value which doesn't fit the other alternatives.
The time zone information, if any, is given as a Time Zone Hint (key -10 or 10),
and the quantization interval is given as a key described in [[#etime-quantization-interval]].</dd>
<dt><code>[etime]</code></dt>
<dd>A local date and time in an unknown time zone, encoded as if it were UTC.
The extended time MUST NOT contain a Time Zone Hint (key -10 or 10),
and its timescale (key -1, -13 or 13), if present, MUST be 0 (UTC).</dd>
</dl>

<p><code>etime</code> or <code>[etime]</code> MUST NOT be used
if another alternative of <code>aqr-time</code> represents the same time value,
the same quantization interval and the same time zone information.
A Time Zone Hint is the same as an <code>aqr-tz-offset</code>
only when it is a numeric offset (e.g. `"+09:00"`) equal to that offset;
an extended time without any quantization interval is never the same as other alternatives.</p>

<div class=note>
  <p>As a consequence, timestamps from common file systems and archive formats never need the extended time,
  and an implementation without full support for [[RFC9581]] can still handle them.
  Such an implementation can ignore an <code>aqr-time</code> using the extended time,
  as an extracting decompressor is allowed to ignore any extra metadata ([[#extraction]]).</p>
</div>

<div class=note>
  Some precisions found in the wild are only representable with the extended time,
  e.g. minutes in ASN.1 `UTCTime`, 2<sup>-32</sup> s in NTP and 1/300 s in SQL Server `DATETIME`,
  and so are time zone offsets that are not multiples of 15 minutes as in UDF.
  They are rare enough in file metadata not to deserve their own encodings.
</div>

<p>Two <code>aqr-time</code> values in different encodings are different even when they denote the same point in time.
For example, `1` and `122(100)` differ in their quantization intervals.
A compressor converting a timestamp from a file system or another archive format
SHOULD preserve its precision, i.e. use an encoding whose quantization interval matches the source.</p>

<div class=note>
  <p>Comparing timestamps from file systems of different precisions is a common source of problems,
  e.g. an unchanged file copied from ext4 to FAT appears to be modified.
  Recording the quantization interval for each timestamp, rather than for each archive,
  lets any consumer tell whether two timestamps can denote the same time,
  even after an archive mixes multiple sources by editing.</p>
</div>

<div class=example>
  <p>The following are possible encodings of 2023-10-19T14:12:34.87Z, or 23:12:34.87 in UTC+9,
  depending on the source:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>ZIP with a DOS date and time, a local time in an unknown time zone: `[121(848878577)]`
    <li>ZIP with an extended timestamp extra field (0x5455) in UTC: `1697724754`
    <li>git, which records a time zone offset in minutes: `[1697724754, 36]`
    <li>exFAT modification time with a UTC offset: `[122(169772475487), 36]`
    <li>NTFS: `125(16977247548700000)`
    <li>ext4: `126(1697724754870000000)`
    <li>FAT access date, a local date in an unknown time zone: `[100(19649)]`
    <li>A source rounding to the nearest second: `1001({1: 1697724755, -14: [0.5, 0.5]})`
  </ul>
</div>

<h5 id=timestamps-from-zip>Timestamps from ZIP</h5>

<p><em>This section is non-normative.</em></p>

<p>A ZIP archive [[APPNOTE]] can carry the same timestamp in multiple places:
the DOS date and time in the local and central headers,
and extra fields 0x000a (NTFS), 0x5455 (extended timestamp), 0x5855 (Info-ZIP UNIX type 1) and 0x000d (UNIX)
[[INFOZIP-EXTRAFLD]].
A compressor converting a ZIP archive can choose the source of each key as follows,
where earlier sources are preferred.
The order prefers more precise sources first, and then more recent specifications for the same precision.</p>

<table class=data>
<thead>
<tr>
  <th>Key</th>
  <th>Sources</th>
</tr>
<tbody>
<tr>
  <td>`crtime`</td>
  <td>0x000a `Ctime`; 0x5455 `CrTime` (local header only)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>`ctime`</td>
  <td>-</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>`mtime`</td>
  <td>0x000a `Mtime`; 0x5455 `ModTime`; 0x5855 or 0x000d `ModTime`; DOS date and time</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>`atime`</td>
  <td>0x000a `Atime`; 0x5455 `AcTime` (local header only); 0x5855 or 0x000d `AcTime`</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>`arctime`</td>
  <td>-</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p>Each source is encoded as follows:</p>

<ul>
  <li>0x000a stores a Windows `FILETIME` |t|, the number of 100 ns intervals since 1601-01-01T00:00:00Z,
  which is encoded as `125(`|t| &minus; 116444736000000000`)`.
  A zero value means that the timestamp is absent.
  <li>0x5455, 0x5855 and 0x000d store the number of seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z,
  which is encoded as an untagged `int`.
  It is a signed 32-bit integer according to [[INFOZIP-EXTRAFLD]],
  though some implementations write times after 2038 as unsigned.
  The central header version of 0x5455 only has `ModTime` even when its flags indicate others,
  so the local header version is preferred when available.
  <li>The DOS date and time is a local time in an unknown time zone with the precision of 2 seconds,
  which is encoded as `[121(`|n|`)]` where 2|n| is the local time as if it were UTC.
</ul>

<p>When both the DOS date and time and a UTC source are present for `mtime`,
their difference reveals the time zone offset where the archive was made.
If the DOS time minus the UTC time differs from 900|k| seconds by less than 2 seconds,
where |k| is an integer in the range of <code>aqr-tz-offset</code>,
`mtime` can be encoded as `[`|u|`, `|k|`]` where |u| is the encoding of the UTC source.
Otherwise, e.g. due to a clock error or a daylight saving time transition, the DOS date and time is simply discarded.</p>

<p>If two sources for the same key disagree by more than their quantization intervals,
a compressor can warn about it, and still uses the preferred source.</p>

<h4 id=comments>Comments</h4>

<p>`archive-comment` of <code>aqr-opt-metadata</code> and `comment` of <code>aqr-entry-extra</code>
are free-form comments attached to the whole archive and to each entry respectively,
e.g. an archive comment and a file comment of ZIP or RAR.
Both are an <code>aqr-text</code>, which is a Unicode string,
a WTF-8 [[WTF-8]] string with lone surrogates, or a byte sequence in an unknown encoding:</p>

<pre class=cddl>
aqr-archive-comment = (
  ? &(archive-comment: -1) => aqr-text
)

aqr-entry-comment = (
  ? &(comment: -6) => aqr-text
)

aqr-text =
  tstr /          ; Unicode
  #6.273(bstr) /  ; WTF-8, i.e. UTF-16 + surrogate compat
  bstr            ; In unknown encoding
</pre>

<p>An <code>aqr-text</code> MUST be a `tstr` if it can be represented as one,
i.e. if it is a sequence of Unicode scalar values.
The byte string in `#6.273(bstr)` MUST be a valid WTF-8 string that is not a valid UTF-8 string,
i.e. it MUST contain at least one encoded lone surrogate.
A `bstr` is not subject to these requirements even if it happens to be a valid UTF-8 string,
as its encoding is not known.</p>

<h5 id=comments-from-zip>Comments from ZIP</h5>

<p><em>This section is non-normative.</em></p>

<p>A ZIP archive [[APPNOTE]] stores a comment for each entry in the central header only,
and a comment for the whole archive in the end of central directory record.
Both are byte sequences, and an empty comment is no different from the absence of one,
so the compressor can omit `comment` or `archive-comment` for an empty comment.</p>

<p>A compressor converting a ZIP archive can determine the encoding of each entry comment
from the following sources, where earlier sources are preferred:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Extra field 0x6375 (Info-ZIP Unicode Comment) of version 1 in the central header [[INFOZIP-EXTRAFLD]],
  whose `ComCRC32` matches the CRC-32 of the comment in the header.
  The field holds the comment in UTF-8.
  A field with a mismatching CRC-32 is ignored, as for 0x7075 in [[#entry-names-from-zip]].
  <li>The comment in the header, if bit 11 (language encoding flag) of the general purpose bit flag is set.
  The comment is in UTF-8.
  <li>Extra field 0x554e (Xceed Unicode) in the central header, which holds the comment in UTF-16LE.
  It is converted to WTF-8, which preserves any lone surrogate.
  <li>Otherwise, the encoding of the comment is not specified.
  The compressor can decode it with the encoding guessed for entry names in the same archive.
  If it can't, the raw byte sequence is used as a `bstr`.
</ol>

<p>The archive comment has neither the language encoding flag nor an extra field,
so its encoding is never specified by the archive itself.
The compressor can decode it as in the last source above, and puts the result to `archive-comment`.</p>

<div class=note>
  <p>Archive comments are often used for purposes other than human-readable text,
  e.g. a self-extracting archive may store its configuration there,
  so decoding one with a guessed encoding can be lossy.
  A compressor unsure about the encoding can keep the raw byte sequence instead.</p>
</div>

<h4 id=link-groups><code>link-group</code>: Hard links</h4>

<pre class=cddl>
aqr-entry-link-group = (
  ? &(link-group: -7) => int  ; File nodes only
)
</pre>

<p><code>link-group</code> records that [=file nodes=] were originally the same file,
i.e. hard links to the same inode on Unix or to the same file record on NTFS.
A non-negative value is <dfn>segment-local</dfn> and denotes the same group only within the same [=segment=],
while a negative value is <dfn>archive-wide</dfn> and denotes the same group in every segment.
The resulting [=file node/link group=] in the [=archive value=] is determined by [=interpret a segment|interpreting a segment=].</p>

<p>A compressor MUST NOT give the same [=file node/link group=] to files that were not the same file.
It SHOULD NOT give a [=file node/link group=] that no other file node has.
A file node with <code>link-group</code> SHOULD refer to the same [=leaf block=] as other file nodes in the same group,
and SHOULD have the same [=entry extra=] as them except for <code>link-group</code> and <code>comment</code>.</p>

<div class=note>
  <p>Segment-local groups allow the inode number (<code>st_ino</code>) to be used as is,
  as CBOR can encode any 64-bit unsigned integer as a non-negative <code>int</code>,
  and can be assigned without reading earlier segments.
  Inode numbers are still only unique within a single file system,
  so a compressor reading from multiple file systems has to renumber some of them.</p>
  <p>Archive-wide groups link files across segments,
  e.g. when a compressor periodically starts a new segment while archiving a single tree,
  or when it appends a segment to an existing archive.
  A compressor using them is responsible for keeping them consistent with all other segments,
  e.g. by reading earlier metadata blocks when appending.
  Inode numbers are not suitable as archive-wide values as is,
  because they can be reused after the file is removed or come from an unrelated file system,
  and linking unrelated files with the same content can't be detected by any decompressor.</p>
</div>

<p>A [=file node/link group=] is <dfn>consistent</dfn> if all file nodes with that group
in the final [=archive value=] have the same [=file node/content=].
An inconsistent group, e.g. after a later segment has replaced only some of its file nodes,
is not an [=invalid archive=] failure, but has no effect on extraction ([[#extraction]]).</p>

<div class=note>
  <p>The consistency can only be determined from the final archive value,
  because a later segment may replace any file node in the group.
  Treating an inconsistent group as an invalid archive would make a decompressor reading only some segments
  unable to detect such failure ([[#partial-access]]).
  A decompressor can instead check the consistency before creating hard links,
  at no cost when file nodes refer to the same leaf block.</p>
  <p>Hard links are not part of the archive model, so a decompressor ignoring <code>link-group</code>
  still extracts the correct files with the same contents.
  Directory hard links, found in some macOS Time Machine volumes, are not represented.</p>
</div>

<h4 id=windows-extras>Windows-specific metadata</h4>

<pre class=cddl>
aqr-entry-windows = (
  ? &(win-link-type: -60) => &(file: 0, directory: 1, junction: 2)  ; Link nodes only

  ; TODO: file attributes (e.g. ZIP external file attributes)
)
</pre>

`win-link-type` is only meaningful for a [=link node=],
and determines the kind of a link to be created on Windows ([[#extraction]]).
"`file`" and "`directory`" denote a symbolic link to a file and to a directory respectively,
and "`junction`" denotes a junction (a mount point reparse point).

<h4 id=unix-extras>Unix-specific metadata</h4>

<pre class=cddl>
aqr-entry-unix = (
  ? &(unix-mode: -70) => uint .le 4095  ; Permission bits only (0o7777), without S_IFMT
  ? &(unix-uid: -71) => uint
  ? &(unix-gid: -72) => uint
)
</pre>

`unix-mode` only holds the permission bits, including the set-user-ID, set-group-ID and sticky bits.
The kind of an entry is solely determined by its node ([[#archive-model]]), so `unix-mode` never includes the file type bits (`S_IFMT`).

`unix-uid` and `unix-gid` are the numeric user and group IDs of the owner.

<h5 id=unix-extras-from-zip>Unix metadata from ZIP</h5>

<p><em>This section is non-normative.</em></p>

<p>A ZIP archive [[APPNOTE]] can carry the Unix metadata of an entry in the external file attributes of the central header
and in extra fields 0x7875 (Info-ZIP UNIX type 3), 0x7855 (Info-ZIP UNIX type 2), 0x756e (ASi UNIX),
0x5855 (Info-ZIP UNIX type 1) and 0x000d (UNIX) [[INFOZIP-EXTRAFLD]].
A compressor converting a ZIP archive can choose the source of each key as follows,
where earlier sources are preferred and sources in the same item have the same precedence.</p>

<table class=data>
<thead>
<tr>
  <th>Key</th>
  <th>Sources</th>
</tr>
<tbody>
<tr>
  <td>`unix-mode`</td>
  <td>External file attributes; 0x756e `Mode`</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>`unix-uid`</td>
  <td>0x7875 `UID`; 0x7855 `UID` (local header only); 0x756e, 0x5855 (local header only) or 0x000d `UID`</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>`unix-gid`</td>
  <td>0x7875 `GID`; 0x7855 `GID` (local header only); 0x756e, 0x5855 (local header only) or 0x000d `GID`</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p>Each source is encoded as follows:</p>

<ul>
  <li>The upper 16 bits of the external file attributes hold the Unix `st_mode`
  if the upper byte of "version made by" is 3 (UNIX).
  Only its permission bits (`0o7777`) go to `unix-mode`, and its file type bits (`S_IFMT`) are not stored there.
  <li>0x7875 of version 1 stores the user and group IDs as little-endian unsigned integers of variable sizes.
  [[INFOZIP-EXTRAFLD]] specifies that it takes precedence over 0x7855.
  Other versions are not used as a source.
  <li>0x7855, 0x756e, 0x5855 and 0x000d store the user and group IDs as 16-bit unsigned integers.
  The central header version of 0x7855 is empty and only indicates that the local header version exists,
  and the central header version of 0x5855 doesn't have the user and group IDs.
</ul>

<p>If two sources for the same key disagree,
a compressor can warn about it, and still uses the preferred source.</p>

<h4 id=zip-extras><code>zip-extras</code>: ZIP extra fields</h4>

<p><code>zip-extras</code> preserves extra fields [[APPNOTE]] of the ZIP entry that the entry was converted from,
when they carry information about the file that has no other representation in this specification.</p>

<pre class=cddl>
aqr-entry-zip = (
  ? &(zip-extras: -100) => aqr-zip-extras
)

aqr-zip-extras = { * aqr-zip-extra-key => bstr .size (0..65531) }
aqr-zip-extra-key =
  0..65535 /   ; Header ID n in the local header
  -65536..-1   ; Header ID n in the central header, as -1 - n
; TODO: multiple extra fields with the same header ID
</pre>

<p>A key |n| from 0 to 65535 denotes the extra field with the header ID |n| in the local header,
and a key &minus;1 &minus; |n| from &minus;65536 to &minus;1 denotes the extra field with the header ID |n| in the central header.
The value is the data of the extra field, excluding its 4-byte header of the header ID and the data size.
Therefore a value with a non-negative key is always in the format for the local header,
and a value with a negative key is always in the format for the central header.</p>

<p>A compressor converting a ZIP archive MAY put any extra field of an entry into `zip-extras`, subject to the following requirements:</p>

* It MUST NOT put the central header version of an extra field
    if the local header version with the same header ID is identical byte by byte.
    The central header version without the local header version is only put with a negative key.
* It MUST NOT put an extra field whose information is fully represented elsewhere,
    i.e. in the entry name, the archive model ([[#archive-model]]) or any other key of <code>aqr-entry-extra</code>.
* It MUST NOT put an extra field which is only meaningful to the ZIP container itself,
    e.g. sizes, offsets, padding, alignment, encryption and signatures over the ZIP archive.
* It SHOULD NOT give `zip-extras` to the root directory, which has no counterpart in ZIP.

<p>Extra fields known to be subject to these requirements are listed in [[#zip-extras-to-remove]].
Any other extra field, including one with an unregistered header ID, can be put into `zip-extras` as is.</p>

<p>`zip-extras` is never authoritative.
If information in a preserved extra field disagrees with the archive model or any other key of <code>aqr-entry-extra</code>,
the latter MUST be used.
A decompressor finding an extra field that a compressor is not allowed to put SHOULD ignore that field only,
and SHOULD NOT treat the whole archive as invalid.</p>

<div class=note>
  <p>`zip-extras` is designed for a one-way conversion from ZIP;
  reconstructing the original ZIP archive from the converted archive is not a goal.
  Extra fields are preserved so that information without its own key yet,
  e.g. platform-specific attributes, access control lists and resource forks,
  can be recovered from already converted archives once a dedicated key is defined.
  Unknown extra fields are preserved for the same reason,
  even though some of them might be only meaningful to the ZIP container.</p>
  <p>The local and central header versions of the same header ID often differ in their formats,
  e.g. the central header version of 0x5455 only has the modification time,
  and that of many Info-ZIP attribute blocks only has a summary.
  Distinguishing them by keys lets a consumer interpret each value in the correct format.
  The key for the central header version is &minus;1 &minus; |n| instead of &minus;|n|,
  so that header ID 0 doesn't collide with the local header version,
  and both versions have the same encoded length in CBOR.</p>
</div>

<h5 id=zip-extras-to-remove>Extra fields not to be preserved</h5>

<p><em>This section is non-normative.</em></p>

<p>The following extra fields [[APPNOTE]] [[INFOZIP-EXTRAFLD]] are not put into `zip-extras`,
because their information is either absorbed into the archive model and other keys,
or discarded as only meaningful to the ZIP container.</p>

<table class=data>
<thead>
<tr>
  <th>Header ID</th>
  <th>Name</th>
  <th>Treatment</th>
</tr>
<tbody>
<tr>
  <td>0x0001</td>
  <td>Zip64 extended information</td>
  <td>Discarded: sizes and offsets</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x0007</td>
  <td>AV Info</td>
  <td>Discarded: signature</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x000a</td>
  <td>NTFS</td>
  <td>Absorbed: timestamps ([[#timestamps-from-zip]])</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x000d</td>
  <td>UNIX (PKWARE)</td>
  <td>Absorbed: timestamps ([[#timestamps-from-zip]]), `unix-uid` and `unix-gid` ([[#unix-extras-from-zip]]), the target of a [=link node=], `link-group` for a hard link, and the device number of a [=special node=]</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x0014</td>
  <td>PKCS#7 Store for X.509 Certificates</td>
  <td>Discarded: signature</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x0015</td>
  <td>X.509 Certificate ID and Signature for individual file</td>
  <td>Discarded: signature</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x0016</td>
  <td>X.509 Certificate ID for Central Directory</td>
  <td>Discarded: signature</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x0017</td>
  <td>Strong Encryption Header</td>
  <td>Discarded: encryption</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x0019</td>
  <td>PKCS#7 Encryption Recipient Certificate List</td>
  <td>Discarded: encryption</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x0021</td>
  <td>Policy Decryption Key Record</td>
  <td>Discarded: encryption</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x0022</td>
  <td>Smartcrypt Key Provider Record</td>
  <td>Discarded: encryption</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x0023</td>
  <td>Smartcrypt Policy Key Data Record</td>
  <td>Discarded: encryption</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x10c5</td>
  <td>minizip CMS signature</td>
  <td>Discarded: signature</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x1986</td>
  <td>Pixar USD header ID</td>
  <td>Discarded: padding for alignment</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x5455</td>
  <td>Extended timestamp</td>
  <td>Absorbed: timestamps ([[#timestamps-from-zip]])</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x554e</td>
  <td>Xceed Unicode</td>
  <td>Absorbed: entry name ([[#entry-names-from-zip]]) and comment ([[#comments-from-zip]])</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x5855</td>
  <td>Info-ZIP UNIX type 1</td>
  <td>Absorbed: timestamps ([[#timestamps-from-zip]]), `unix-uid` and `unix-gid` ([[#unix-extras-from-zip]])</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x5a4c</td>
  <td>ZipArchive code page</td>
  <td>Absorbed: entry name ([[#entry-names-from-zip]])</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x5a4d</td>
  <td>ZipArchive offsets array</td>
  <td>Discarded: offsets</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x6375</td>
  <td>Info-ZIP Unicode Comment</td>
  <td>Absorbed: comment ([[#comments-from-zip]])</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x6c78</td>
  <td>libarchive "xl"</td>
  <td>Absorbed: a copy of the central header fields</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x7075</td>
  <td>Info-ZIP Unicode Path</td>
  <td>Absorbed: entry name ([[#entry-names-from-zip]])</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x7855</td>
  <td>Info-ZIP UNIX type 2</td>
  <td>Absorbed: `unix-uid` and `unix-gid` ([[#unix-extras-from-zip]])</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x7875</td>
  <td>Info-ZIP UNIX type 3</td>
  <td>Absorbed: `unix-uid` and `unix-gid` ([[#unix-extras-from-zip]]), for version 1 only</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x9901</td>
  <td>AE-x (WinZip AES)</td>
  <td>Discarded: encryption</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0x9903</td>
  <td>WinZip Reference</td>
  <td>Discarded: reference to another entry, resolved by conversion</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0xa11e</td>
  <td>Data Stream Alignment (Apache Commons Compress)</td>
  <td>Discarded: padding for alignment</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0xa220</td>
  <td>Microsoft Open Packaging Growth Hint</td>
  <td>Discarded: padding for in-place editing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0xcafe</td>
  <td>Java JAR marker</td>
  <td>Discarded: information about the ZIP file itself</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0xcdcd</td>
  <td>minizip Central Directory</td>
  <td>Discarded: encryption and signature of the central directory</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0xd935</td>
  <td>Android ZIP Alignment</td>
  <td>Discarded: padding for alignment</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>0xe57a</td>
  <td>ALZip code page</td>
  <td>Absorbed: entry name ([[#entry-names-from-zip]]), if decoded with that code page</td>
</tr>
</table>

<div class=note>
  <p>Some extra fields are preserved only in particular cases.
  The local and central header versions are considered separately.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>0x756e (ASi UNIX) is absorbed like 0x000d:
    the permission bits of its mode go to `unix-mode`, the user and group IDs go to `unix-uid` and `unix-gid`,
    and the symbolic link target goes to a [=link node=].
    Its device number, however, is a raw `st_rdev` value whose bit layout differs among platforms,
    and can't be split into the major and minor numbers of a [=special node=].
    A device file is therefore converted to an empty [=file node=] with the original extra field preserved.
    <li>0x4b46 (FWKCS MD5) and 0x1a51 (minizip hash) hold digests of the uncompressed content.
    They are only preserved when they match the actual content.
  </ul>
</div>

<h3 id=formula-data>
Formula data</h3>

This section describes the internal structure of [=formula/formula_data=] in formula blocks ([[#formula-block]]),
after it has been decompressed.

A formula consists of the lists of blocks it references, followed by [=formula/instructions=] of a stack machine.
Each instruction pops zero or more byte sequences from the stack and pushes zero or more byte sequences,
and the byte sequences remaining on the stack after the last instruction are the outputs of the formula.

<div dfn-for=formula>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_blocks</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>block_ordinal_deltas</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=][[=formula/num_blocks=]]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_resources</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>resource_indices</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=][[=formula/num_resources=]]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_modules</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>module_indices</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=][[=formula/num_modules=]]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>instructions</dfn>
      <td>[=formula/instruction=][*]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

[=formula/block_ordinal_deltas=] lists the non-leaf blocks referenced by the formula block.
Their <em>0-based</em> ordinals among the non-leaf blocks following the formula block within the [=segment=]
are |o|<sub>0</sub> = [=formula/block_ordinal_deltas=][0] and
|o|<sub>|i|</sub> = |o|<sub>|i| - 1</sub> + [=formula/block_ordinal_deltas=][|i|] + 1 for |i| ≥ 1,
so the ordinals are strictly increasing.
The |i|-th <dfn>referenced block</dfn> is the non-leaf block with the ordinal |o|<sub>|i|</sub>, which MUST exist.

[=formula/resource_indices=] and [=formula/module_indices=] list the resource and module blocks
referenced by the formula block, as <em>0-based</em> indices within the [=segment=] ([[#content-block]]).
Each of them MUST refer to an existing block, which precedes the formula block by [[#block-order]].

[=formula/instructions=] fill the rest of [=formula/formula_data=].
Each block listed in the above lists MUST be used by at least one instruction,
and is said to be referenced by the formula block only in that case.

<div class=note>
  Non-leaf blocks are referenced by ordinals rather than by offsets,
  so that relocating or recompressing a block, or inserting other blocks such as mirror blocks,
  doesn't invalidate formula blocks referencing blocks after it.
  An offset would also make the result depend on how the block is located:
  an offset pointing into the middle of a block can still find a valid-looking block there,
  e.g. in an archive stored inside an uncompressed block,
  while a decompressor reading blocks in order would find no block at that offset.
  Ordinals in a typical parcel are consecutive, so [=formula/block_ordinal_deltas=] are mostly zero.
  Resources and modules are referenced by absolute indices,
  which recur in many formula blocks and compress well with a shared dictionary.
</div>

Each <dfn>instruction</dfn> consists of an <dfn>opcode</dfn> of type [=u8=] followed by its operands,
whose structure is determined by the opcode as described in the following sections.
[=formula/opcode=] MUST be one of the following, and its meaning is defined in [[#formula-evaluation]]:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Opcode
      <th>Instruction
      <th>Pops
      <th>Pushes
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>00<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#insn-nop|Nop]]
      <td>0
      <td>0
    <tr>
      <td>01<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#insn-literal|Literal]]
      <td>0
      <td>1
    <tr>
      <td>0F<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#insn-swap|Swap]]
      <td>0
      <td>0
    <tr>
      <td>10<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#insn-slice|Slice]]
      <td>0
      <td>1
    <tr>
      <td>11<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#insn-apply|Apply]]
      <td>[=formula/apply_num_inputs=]
      <td>[=formula/apply_num_outputs=]
    <tr>
      <td>12<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#insn-concat|Concatenate]]
      <td>[=formula/concat_count=]
      <td>1
    <tr>
      <td>13<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#insn-pack|Pack]]
      <td>[=formula/pack_count=]
      <td>1
    <tr>
      <td>14<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#insn-split|Split]]
      <td>1
      <td>[=formula/split_count=]
    <tr>
      <td>20<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#insn-checksum|Checksum]]
      <td>0
      <td>0
  </tbody>
</table>

Opcodes are grouped by their roles:
00<sub>16</sub> through 0F<sub>16</sub> load data or rearrange the stack,
10<sub>16</sub> through 1F<sub>16</sub> compute byte sequences,
and 20<sub>16</sub> through 2F<sub>16</sub> check them.
Other opcodes are reserved.

Issue: Opcode 02<sub>16</sub> is reserved for a reference to another file,
which may instead be declared as a list along with other referenced blocks.

[=formula/formula_data=] is not randomly accessible,
and a decompressor is expected to read and decompress it in its entirety before evaluating any part of the formula.
Every requirement on [=formula/formula_data=] that doesn't depend on the evaluated byte sequences,
such as indices into the lists and the number of byte sequences on the stack,
can be checked in a single pass over [=formula/instructions=] without evaluating anything.
The same pass determines the length of every byte sequence on the stack,
so [=formula/formula_data=] then serves as an index for random access within the output,
in the same way as a seek table of other formats.
The [=referenced blocks=] are located by skipping blocks following the formula block by their [=block/size=] fields.

<div class=note>
  The index grows linearly with the number of non-leaf blocks referenced,
  but it compresses well because most instructions share the same shape and sizes.
  The decompression of the whole [=formula/formula_data=] is a one-time cost per file.
</div>

<h4 id=insn-nop>
Nop instruction</h4>

Opcode 00<sub>16</sub> denotes a **Nop instruction**, which has no operands.

<div class=note>
  Nop instructions can pad [=formula/formula_data=],
  e.g. to keep it compressible when its original size would otherwise be smaller than its compressed size.
</div>

<h4 id=insn-literal>
Literal instruction</h4>

Opcode 01<sub>16</sub> denotes a **Literal instruction**. Its operands consist of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>literal_size</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>literal_data</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=formula/literal_size=]]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h4 id=insn-swap>
Swap instruction</h4>

Opcode 0F<sub>16</sub> denotes a **Swap instruction**. Its operands consist of the following:

<table class=data>
